      "correct": true,
      "hit_at_5": true,
      "citation_precision": 1.0,
      "mode": "dense",
      "answer": "They scale the dot products by \\(1/\\sqrt{d_k}\\) because, as the dimensionality \\(d_k\\) grows, the dot products become large in magnitude, which pushes the softmax into regions with extremely small gradients. Scaling by \\(1/\\sqrt{d_k}\\) counteracts this effect and keeps the softmax well\u2011behaved[3].",
      "route_got": "document_question",
      "latency": 6.32,
